","params":{"name":"sayHours","fn":"round","a":"sayHours"}},{"id":"1c2d6525-b192-4034-be8a-f0d62cf081cb","type":"calc","params":{"name":"sayHours","a":"sayHours","op":"/","b":10}},{"id":"ca0c7ad2-3e2b-408b-bfd3-143478473a3d","type":"say","params":{"text":"Day {yearDay}, latitude {latitude}: noon Sun {sayNoon} degrees up, {sayHours} hours of daylight."}}]}]}],"homeFromRecord":true,"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"se-daylight-column","options":{}},{"id":"f224e4b0-0ba6-426e-b679-773ca8f0befc","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[13,0,148],"rotation":[0,-2.854311200192659,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"block-board","options":{"number":3,"title":"The dial owns the day and the place","steps":[{"cat":"math","text":"set yearDay to 172","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"set latitude to 37.5","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"share yearDay with every object","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"share latitude with every object","depth":0}],"caption":"Click the Season Dial, press Program, change a green number.","accent":"#e0a02e"}},{"id":"c2d6b28f-91bb-4dc7-a0e4-4bd596fc267a","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[-22,0,151],"rotation":[0,2.7038013499482467,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"block-board","options":{"number":4,"title":"Working out the noon Sun","steps":[{"cat":"math","text":"set a to yearDay − 81","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"set a to a × 0.9863","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"set s to sin° of a","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"set declination to s × 23.44","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"set gap to latitude − declination","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"set gap to absolute of gap","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"set noon to 90 − gap","depth":0}],"caption":"This is in the gold column. Click it to open the whole program.","accent":"#e0a02e"}},{"id":"04d12d86-6e89-42fd-abb5-9c849f43fad9","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[62,0,131],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"se-season-orrery","options":{}},{"id":"96655a8f-73da-4477-9900-c871f1919a6e","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[-62,0,131],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"se-sky-bowl","options":{}},{"id":"e8b4ff37-df84-4555-8b7c-8e95f83c94ac","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[22,0,97],"rotation":[0,0.722979353401491,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"THE COMMON WRONG ANSWER","title":"Not how close we are","body":"The Earth is closest to the Sun in early January: 91.4 million miles, against 94.5 in early July. If distance made the seasons, the whole world would have its summer together. Australia has summer when Kansas has winter.","accent":"#e0a02e","width":3.4,"height":4.2}},{"id":"459d6871-a921-4a8e-b4e5-1ee2bf87ea0e","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[60,0,85],"rotation":[0,0.04345089539153084,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"WATCH THE RED TIP","title":"The axis never swings","body":"The Earth leans 23.44 degrees and keeps leaning the same way all year. In June that tips the north toward the Sun; in December the same lean tips it away. The gold ring is the latitude of Kansas: see how much of it is lit.","accent":"#e0a02e","width":3.4,"height":4.2}},{"id":"f86e9850-54ae-4f42-8d28-45d402c1ca9d","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[-60,0,97],"rotation":[0,-0.058755822715722696,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"THE SAME THING FROM THE GROUND","title":"High Sun, long day","body":"At Girard, Kansas the noon Sun stands 76 degrees up in June and 29 in December, with 14.6 hours of daylight against 9.4. Stand at the stick and face south. Gold is June, pale blue March and September, blue December, white today.","accent":"#e0a02e","width":3.4,"height":4.2}},{"id":"b7d63b9b-5301-4ba2-9074-0e3b6cfa10c1","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[-18,0,112],"rotation":[0,0.5713374798336268,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"activity-board","options":{"number":3,"title":"June and December","target":"Click the Season Dial, press Program:","steps":[{"cat":"math","text":"set yearDay to 172","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"then 355","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"then 81","depth":0}],"tip":"Read the gold column each time. Then set latitude to your own town.","accent":"#e0a02e"}},{"id":"e121a2db-83b1-48c2-b1a0-607f0083ce64","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[18,0,112],"rotation":[0,-0.5713374798336268,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"activity-board","options":{"number":4,"title":"Go somewhere else","target":"Change latitude on the Season Dial:","steps":[{"cat":"math","text":"set latitude to 0","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"then -33.9","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"then 70","depth":0}],"tip":"At 70 the column has no answer: acos only takes -1 to 1. Open it, and just before acos add: if x < -1, set x to -1. If x > 1, set x to 1.","accent":"#e0a02e"}},{"id":"f4f15478-e0ff-4806-a141-b48c22d9102c","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[0,0,176],"rotation":[0,3.141592653589793,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"se-calendar-post","options":{}},{"id":"5a16fb50-7093-49dc-96fb-700e886246d1","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[16,0,174],"rotation":[0,-2.5535900500422257,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"activity-board","options":{"number":5,"title":"Run the whole year","target":"The Calendar has no program. Click it and build:","steps":[{"cat":"control","text":"repeat 365 times","depth":0},{"cat":"math","text":"change yearDay by 1","depth":1},{"cat":"math","text":"share yearDay","depth":1},{"cat":"control","text":"wait 0.05 seconds","depth":1}],"tip":"Watch the Earth, the white path and the column. The Season Dial’s green arrow takes the day back.","accent":"#e0a02e"}},{"id":"9859ab03-6910-4104-b46e-5808b868d535","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[-30.166077292245518,0,-150],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"se-true-scale-earth","options":{"radius":1}},{"id":"85491e87-5e04-4c5c-99fc-2529794d7be3","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[30.166077292245518,0,-150],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"se-true-scale-moon","options":{"radius":0.272796160646628}},{"id":"33c24a1a-d798-4894-b3bf-4e69394e60b2","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[0,0,-134],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"standing-sign","options":{"lines":["THE REAL DISTANCES"],"subtitle":"This Earth and Moon are to scale","width":13,"height":3.2,"postHeight":9.5,"face":"#18202b","accent":"#3f9fc9"}},{"id":"456c4411-ba62-4535-9f12-4718646b5d69","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[-26.166077292245518,0,-143],"rotation":[0,0.18097748509002803,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"THE TRUE SCALE","title":"An Earth one foot in radius","body":"Then the Moon is 3.3 inches in radius and 60 feet away, which is this gap. Almost every picture of the two gets that wrong, and so does the lab on the pole, to fit the world.","accent":"#3f9fc9","width":3.4,"height":4.2}},{"id":"6c5e4194-168b-464e-869b-c04ba1604580","createdAt":1793397714007,"transform":{"position":[26.166077292245518,0,-143],"rotation":[0,-0.18097748509002803,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"AND THE SUN?","title":"109 feet in radius","body":"At this scale the Sun is 4.4 miles away. Real sizes: Earth 3,959 miles in radius, Moon 1,080, 238,855 miles apart. The globe you stand on is 110 feet in radius.","accent":"#3f9fc9","width":3.4,"height":4.2}}]}